00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.000
What is the data and graphics department?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.000
It is a team of people who try to create digital journalism, and I would underline digital, right?

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:19.000
That is important to our readers with data, development, and design.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
That's pretty broad.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.000
What's it really boil down to?

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:37.000
It boils down to a group of about 15 to 20 people who are reporters and editors, just like any other department in the newsroom, but who are also computer programmers, information designers, and just in some cases, nerds, right?

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:50.000
And so we are journalists who have a lot of the traditional skills of conventional reporters, but we also have a different set of skills that allows us to gather, organize, analyze data, right?

00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:03.000
To find stories in the raw materials of data, and then to convert that raw material into a new line of finished products, a different assembly line than what you might have found in a newspaper 100 years ago.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.000
And so on those assembly lines, on those assembly lines, we make roughly three types of things, I would say.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
One, we make what I would call applications.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:17.000
These are software products that the data is the raw material, and the thing on the web is the product for the audience.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.000
Thing two is visual stories.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.000
This is using visualization techniques to help people understand the news, and three is digital designs.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.000
This is using our computer skills to break outside the mold of traditional storytelling forms and do different things online.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.000
These are the three types of things we make, and we're going to go through them now, kind of one, two, three, with some concrete examples.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.000
All right?

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.000
So thing one is what I would call applications.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Some people call them news apps.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.000
It's not really a great term for it, but you'll know when you see it.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.000
They gather and refine data to serve the audience.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
A classic example right now for us is our coronavirus tracker.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:09.000
So there are dozens of pages on the L.A. Times website that are gathering data from all across the internet about the latest on the spread of coronavirus in California, and converting that into a series of web pages that allow people to keep tabs on what's happening.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.000
We're not the only newsroom doing this.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.000
The one in your community, I'm sure, is as well, wherever you happen to be on planet Earth.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:21.000
This has been a huge feature for data journals and departments around the world the last couple of years, right?

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Another one would be something like live election results.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:38.000
So in our governor's recall election last week, our team was responsible for writing the computer code and building the systems that ferry the latest election results live onto the internet and visualize them in tables and charts and maps, right?

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Another example would be our live wildfires map.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:55.000
This is where we take in data that's that's sprinkled around government websites all over the internet and written computer code that pulls it together and gives you a dashboard of the latest view of what's happening with wildfires here in the West.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Another example would be our quick black tool.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:11.000
This is where every time there's an earthquake, a little pulse of data is sent out by government seismologists, and we have computer code that's reading that in evaluating according to our algorithm, whether or not it's news.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:17.000
And then if the answer is yes, spitting out this post automatically with no human intervention, right?

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.000
Another example would be our database of police killings.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:37.000
We're a team at the L.A. Times with rebuild the database of everyone in L.A. County has been killed by police match that up with later investigations into those killings and then created this online database, which is its own feature on the web, independent of any traditional story.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:51.000
And this type of approach of application building can be applied in really any circumstance where there's sort of a moving, flowing data feed that itself is generating news right based on the latest things that kind of show up in it.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.000
I think stock prices right would be like an example from a totally different domain, right?

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:07.000
But then also can be rolled up into something like a tracker or an app or a thing that then summarizes that data and helps readers like kind of get their grip on it, right?

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.000
And they themselves on their own can be very, very popular features for readers.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Our coronavirus tracker is the most popular thing ever published by the Los Angeles Times on the Internet, just on its own, without ever having generated a traditional front page story or scoop.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:33.000
Or like, you know, conventional data journalism product and that's not just true for us that's true for all the other newsrooms that are doing the same thing.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.000
However, once you have assembled that data.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:50.000
It can become a powerful resource for you to do enterprise and investigative work, whereby having assembled all the information you can then begin to ask interesting questions and tell stories that other people can't because you have it together.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:09:13.000
And so these data applications tend to be a great partner for analysis and enterprise like, for instance, in the case of our COVID tracker having assembled all this data from all these different domains, not just cases and deaths, but openings and closings and other elements on the calendar allowed us to do a lot of enterprise pieces about how the government was handling the response and whether it was going well or not.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:38.000
Right. In the case of the police killings database, when one particular killing drew a lot of media attention, a young man who was shot after a bicycle stop, we were able to look into our database and identify many other cases that had similar characteristics and to provide context about the one event that was in the news because we had this data to draw from.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.000
Right.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.000
Iris has been a leader on our team on exactly these kinds of efforts and even on this particular story.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:54.000
And so now I want to hand it off to her so that she can kind of walk through all the steps to go into creating a piece like these ones we've been looking at.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Right, and the one we're going to look at is her recent standalone tracker about coronavirus cases identified at the L.A. Unified School District, America's second largest school district, which just reopened iris take it away.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Okay, thanks, Ben.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:18.000
So, just to go back one slide to the design because the story.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:27.000
Like, I'm just going to kind of repeat what Ben said so we love to collect data and we like to think about how to present what we collected with our readers a lot.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:42.000
So, at times, it can be like a very straightforward presentation, like the police killings database that you saw, or we can dig deeper and give more context and focus on the stories that bring the data home to a person.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:11:04.000
Or a community, and I think like this is a great example of that we were able to use the data we collected and our reporters reported out and found very similar cases and wrote like this beautiful story about others like these on kids who was killed by the police on while riding a bike.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:13.000
So, I just want you guys to keep this in mind well while I talk about the LAUSD tracker because I also keep this in mind whenever i'm about to jump into a data project.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:19.000
Okay, so the LAUSD tracker next site.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:27.000
So we're going to go deeper into the application that we published recently, this is the LAUSD outbreak tracker.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:43.000
So when schools started for the Los Angeles unified school district back in late August, there was a lot of anxiety around you know kids going back to school, given that you know only if you were eligible eligible for vaccines.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:54.000
There was like a delta surge happening, and I knew that people were kind of like talking about this next slide and an education reporter.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:03.000
So, and I think that's what I was working with at the time on a different project kind of showed me this dashboard that the district had published.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:14.000
So LAUSD district published this dashboard where you could kind of scroll down to a specific school and see if there were any active COVID cases among staff and students.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:26.000
So, as well as if there was an outbreak in the school or if the school was being investigated for an outbreak, as well as all these other information around case rates and the case rates in the community.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:35.000
And she kind of—our educational reporter, Paloma—she was like, “Oh, do you think you could collect this data?”

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:44.000
I think my initial response was like, well, do we really need to because when we collect this type of data.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:50.000
The question I like to ask for us is like, do we have to like, are we going to make this better by collecting this data?

00:12:50.000 --> 00:13:03.000
And that's what kind of I asked myself first, because even though we do this a lot, this takes a lot of work and a lot of programming and a lot of cleaning.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:12.000
And especially if we're going to collect data over and over again, over a period of time, we're going to have to maintain a which takes a teamwork, you know, all the coronavirus trackers.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:17.000
It takes a village to run this sort of application and maintain it, especially over time.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:24.000
So I was kind of thinking about if we need it or not, because the information presented there is pretty, you know, it's good.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.000
It's good information.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.000
It's pretty straightforward.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:45.000
And then my second reason for being hesitant was that us presenting this information wouldn't make it any more clear that, you know, the 75th Street Elementary or Early Education Center has like zero cases today.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:53.000
But then what this doesn't show this dashboard doesn't show is it doesn't show the cumulus has numbers, right?

00:13:53.000 --> 00:14:02.000
So even though we could kind of look at every school, we don't know what it looks like from bird's eye point of view when we look at the school district as a whole.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:03.000
So it doesn't show that.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:18.000
And also because this data refreshes every day, we don't know, we can't see the trend, you know, is the case counts rising or the case is the case count dropping is, is there more outbreaks or less outbreaks than a week before.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:31.000
So this was kind of like myself process in deciding that I was going to dig into this data and try to pry it out from the web.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:38.000
And also, lastly, for you know programmers out there, if you ever try to scrape any kind of dashboard.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:49.000
This is a Microsoft BI dashboard, which is very notorious for having very complicated like data back in and it's very hard to pry data out of this dashboard.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:53.000
So I just knew it was going to be a bit of work.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:15:11.000
So when we scrape data, which is like our term for using computer programming to take data out of a site, we can do it in largely two ways, we can kind of look in the back side of the site and see where the data feed is coming from.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:17.000
This is like your API's, and we can try to like download that or connect to that and we could get the data that way.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:31.000
Or for this instance, I decided to go with web scraping using a Python library called Selenium, which allows you to control the web server or the web browser.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:43.000
You can tell you can write a code in Python and tell the code to, you know, click on this part and then save that number or click and drag on this part and save that number.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:55.000
And I decided to go with selenium because I've actually had scrape a very similar dashboard just couple months prior for the California prison system.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.000
So I was already a little familiar with how this would work out.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.000
So I kind of like try to give it a go.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:07.000
I started my Jupyter Notebook and started tinkering with some code to see if I could start collecting the data.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:16.000
And once I kind of wrote a script that I knew that was going to collect data for every school in that dashboard by clicking.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:24.000
You know, drop pointing to the drop down clicking the school taking the information pointing the drop down clicking the school and taking another information.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:41.000
So I knew that I knew that I could, you know, collect this information and I, and what we usually do after that in order to run the script daily or monthly or weekly, we put it on a thing called cron, which is basically like a scheduler.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:56.000
So, so we can tell GitHub Actions to run my scraper script every day at, you know, 4am when, you know, hopefully there's no traffic and hopefully no one's like messing in the repository.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:08.000
So we put it on a cron and we set a scheduled run every day for my script to collect the data from the dashboard once it refreshes.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:13.000
So once we got the data that way, I knew that we could get it daily.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:19.000
We took that data back into the notebook, and this is so we just collected the data.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:37.000
And now, with the data that we collected, we start cleaning up the data right, so we have to kind of append the data on top of each other so yesterday's data on top of today's data, so that we can kind of see the time trend and I did that using again Python.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:52.000
pandas library, which is like a data analysis library that you can use to kind of collect and simplify the data and kind of trim down to the information that we want to show our readers that we think will be helpful for our readers.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:05.000
And in the back of my head, I was also thinking, well, now that we're aggregating this data every day, perhaps in, you know, maybe at the end of the semester, I can look back at all this data that we collected.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:11.000
And see if there is another story because this application is just presentation of data.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.000
It's not really digging in deep into the story.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:23.000
And in my mind, I was thinking, well, but because we're doing this collection, there is an opportunity to dig into this data later on, maybe after the semester.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:45.000
So once we process all this data, we feed it, we feed the data out into a different format called JSON format from like basically a data frame to a JSON file so that our our front end publishing system can read this data.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:53.000
So we have a special publishing system that was built by our team.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:57.000
And it can read this data in a JSON format and build out a page for us.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:09.000
So it uses front end code like HTML or front end languages like HTML, CSS and JavaScript, and it kind of takes the data.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:13.000
We can make charts with it or tables with it.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:21.000
We can also, you know, write, you know, paragraphs, insert like pieces of data in our paragraph using like templating.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:30.000
And so we, and that's what we did so we fed the data into our system to kind of work on the presentation.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.000
So our final project was this outbreak dashboard.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:49.000
So what this dashboard does is every day it updates with new data collected from the LAUSD, LAUSD's own dashboard, and you can kind of see the time trend where active cases are going.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:20:03.000
You can kind of see how many schools have how many cases, and then parents can look for schools, or there are markers where the schools are bolded, which means they're also being investigated by the outbreak.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:13.000
So it's kind of a place where readers could come every day and suss out the trends happening in their communities or in their school district.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:34.000
But for me, my hidden motive or my secondary motive also is to kind of collect this data daily, and eventually look back and do a look back into the first semester that LAUSD open to see if there's anything that the data could tell us that we can't get.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:49.000
We like that we can easily get so that oh, and then I also want to say that I was able to do this fairly quickly with a lot of help from Ben in about a week.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:21:02.000
And that's only because we were we have done this before, and that's kind of a privilege I have as a journalist and a large data team with a lot of programmers and reporters that I can learn from.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:20.000
Because we've done the coronavirus tracker for the last year and a half for me to make this page was very easy, because all the pieces were there I knew what we had to do this was, you know that probably like the 50s or 60s page that we're doing that's in similar format.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:32.000
But it's probably good to note that there are a lot of data journalists like me out there that are, you know, only ones in the newsroom that kind of have to do this old on older by themselves from scratch.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:39.000
So I was really lucky that I had a big team that worked on this project, a very supportive editor as well.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:43.000
Yeah, and that's the story. Ben.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:50.000
Thank you, Iris, and we can go into all the nerdy stuff later if people want to know about code, but I'm going to head, I'm going to zoom back out to the high level.

00:21:50.000 --> 00:22:06.000
So I was screen gave us a great example of like assembly line one, which are these data applications that sometimes lead to data enterprise and analysis right now I'm going to jump ahead to think to so that the second type of thing we make is what I would call a visual story.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:15.000
There's not really a name for this type of thing, but pretty much this has become the stock and trade of I think most digital graphics departments around the world.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:31.000
This is where graphics reporters, you know, are making and reporting and designing visual coverage of major events where their work is substantial enough to stand alone, as opposed to charts that are just an accessory to a traditional story in print.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:40.000
The web is this whole new opportunity for visual journalists to do their own thing and to go far above and beyond what words alone can do.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:52.000
And examples of that would be when Kobe Bryant's helicopter crashed a few years ago here in Southern California, an unexpected event on a Sunday morning, they kick started a whole cycle of coverage by our team.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:23:11.000
And you know, one or two days later we had pieced together a visual piece that used the available data on the web to guide you through the final moments of the helicopter flight to help readers understand the sort of fatal final turn and mistake by the pilot that led to led to the crash.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:20.000
And this is a case where we're using the same types of tools that I was just talking about in terms of building a page, but there's not this like data pipeline behind it all.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:32.000
This is really a case where those tools are being used to showcase visual storytelling techniques and and the piece like this can come together in only one or two days if we really get our act together.

