00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:42.720
The next question is from Cindy Broyal, who mentioned how important the visual can be to telling a story, and how important is it to make the presentations interactive and engaging the audience.

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:45.720
The next question is, Iris, do you want to jump in and then Ben?

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:50.720
Yeah, I think we talk about this a lot in our team, too.

00:49:50.720 --> 00:49:55.720
Sometimes we think less is more. Sometimes it doesn't have to have large bells or whistles.

00:49:55.720 --> 00:50:01.720
Sometimes us, you know, a simple bar graph can tell you a lot more than you think.

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:12.720
And then other times, like the Kobe story, like visual elements are like the only way you can understand a story really fully.

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:19.720
So I sorry I kind of lost my train of thought. Was there a question again?

00:50:19.720 --> 00:50:26.720
No worries. So looking at how how important is it to make the presentation interactive and engaging the audience?

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:31.720
Right. So interactivity also is like a hard technical lift.

00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:41.720
So we always think about is it worth it? Is it really enhancing the story, helping the story by putting this feature and asking audience to engage?

00:50:41.720 --> 00:50:44.720
Will it help our readers?

00:50:44.720 --> 00:50:48.720
And a big example of that, which we always try to make it interactive is like the precincts map.

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:53.720
You know, everyone wants to know where they're like how the area that they're living voted.

00:50:53.720 --> 00:51:05.720
So we like to make those things interactive and other times we try to, you know, dial it down and just keep it simple because that's enough.

00:51:05.720 --> 00:51:11.720
Yep. And one thing I would just add is that, you know, one big change since I started doing this work is just the rise of mobile phones.

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:26.720
When I started in the flash era when everybody was on desktop computers, graphics tended to be very heavily interactive because there was this expectation that all of your users had like a mouse and that interactivity was how you really went above and beyond a traditional piece.

00:51:26.720 --> 00:51:38.720
So for now with, you know, 60% or more of your readers looking at things on mobile phones, they just do all those bells and whistles and those more sort of detailed exquisite displays just no longer work.

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:56.720
And so I think that's a big reason why over the last 10 years you've seen the top graphics groups in the world really simplifying a lot moving towards scrolling type approaches and thumbing type approaches over click and pick kind of approaches.

00:51:56.720 --> 00:51:58.720
So, thank you.

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:14.720
Thank you, Ben and Iris. So we have one last question. So in your opinion, what are the fundamental computer coding skills or data science skills one need to learn if they want to break out into the data journalism field.

00:52:14.720 --> 00:52:20.720
So we start with iris. Yeah, I think that depends.

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:30.720
You can, if you start with Python, you can do more ambitious analysis that you might not be able to do in itself. Same for R.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:42.720
I like Python because it's kind of more flexible in terms of I can make a tool and I can also do like analysis. But if you are into more visual storytelling.

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:52.720
Front-end computer languages like HTML, CSS, and JavaScript are probably what you want to learn.

00:52:52.800 --> 00:53:01.119
When I got started and thought about learning these programming languages,

00:53:01.119 --> 00:53:21.599
I still think about this a lot because I think the best way to learn is kind of like pick a project that you want to try.

00:53:21.599 --> 00:53:35.680
You know, like, I like this story because of the analysis, or maybe I like the story because of this beautiful graphic. You know, I want to do something like this. And then, like, pick a goal and try to learn things around it because depending on what you want to do, there are so many languages you can learn and so many languages you don't have to learn.

00:53:35.680 --> 00:53:49.720
So I would recommend kind of really thinking about what is it? Do I just want to learn programming, and, but why? You know, like, what do I want to do with it? And then maybe setting like a post to go towards, walk towards.

00:53:52.720 --> 00:53:53.720
Thank you, Iris.

00:53:53.720 --> 00:53:55.720
So you're saying that practice is really important.

00:53:55.720 --> 00:54:00.720
So you can start with a project that you're interesting to learn more about the coding stuff, right?

00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:01.720
Thank you.

00:54:01.720 --> 00:54:03.720
What do you say, Ben?

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:06.720
I think Iris it on.

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:15.720
I think really having an applied approach and an incremental attitude like you're gonna like gradually learn more and more as you do more and more projects is totally right.

00:54:15.720 --> 00:54:17.720
It takes a long time to get good at all this stuff.

00:54:17.720 --> 00:54:20.720
But no one is just gonna study, study, study, and then be ready, right?

00:54:20.720 --> 00:54:24.720
It's gonna be fits and starts through your whole career, and you have to embrace that process.

00:54:24.720 --> 00:54:30.720
The only thing I would add is that if you're interested in working with data, in my view, the spreadsheet is the fundamental skill.

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:43.720
You know, I think that being able to structure data into a simple spreadsheet, sort it, filter it, group and analyze it with pivot tables.

00:54:43.720 --> 00:54:47.720
It, look at the data and find flaws in it, right?

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:48.720
You know what I mean?

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:49.720
Correct those flaws.

00:54:49.720 --> 00:54:51.720
Calculate new columns.

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:57.720
Those four or five like basic skills to me are, it's like learning how to dribble in basketball.

00:54:57.720 --> 00:55:00.720
You know, you can't really play until you understand those basic skills.

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:14.720
And then the computer programming of, of R or of Python just allows you to scale up and to do those skills like more effectively with larger data sets and script it so it can work while you're sleeping like Iris has done or whatever else.

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:28.720
But it all comes back to the fundamentals of structured data, which is found in LibreOffice, let's say the free alternative to Microsoft Excel.

00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:29.720
Thank you, Ben.

00:55:29.720 --> 00:55:34.720
I put in the chat for everyone some resources that you wanted to leave folks with as well.

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:39.720
Would you be able to talk for just a second about the Python first Python notebook then?

00:55:39.720 --> 00:55:41.720
And what people may be able to get out of that?

00:55:41.720 --> 00:55:42.720
Yeah, sure.

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:58.720
I mean, so five or six years ago I came to San Diego State for an IRE training event and we, we did an experiment, we said, could we sort of like tack on a Python and Jupyter Notebook training class on to an event that was otherwise happening there.

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:16.720
And with some partners of mine, including Cheryl Phillips at Stanford, and Aaron Williams, who's now at Netflix and others, we developed a little bit of a class that teaches you just enough Python and Jupyter Notebook to do an analysis of campaign finance and California politics.

00:56:16.720 --> 00:56:23.720
And so this class which is at firstpythonnotebook.org, and has improved quite a bit since our first effort I think.

00:56:23.720 --> 00:56:40.720
It's the idea is to help you to learn how to dribble with Python, but then also at the same time to learn some of the important caveats and quirks of analyzing money and politics, which has its own kind of interesting challenges.

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:44.720
Thanks, Ben, you think that will be really helpful for folks if they want to dive into it.

